# nginx allocates two things once and lets the process exit take them back,
# so valgrind calls them lost.  Neither comes from this module: both traces
# end in nginx's own code, and the module appears in neither.
#
# These two are the only entries, and that is measured, not assumed.  With
# an empty file the suite reports exactly these and nothing else -- no
# invalid read, no invalid write, no uninitialised value, no conditional
# jump, no mismatched free.  Anything in here beyond them would suppress
# something that never happens, and would hide whatever lands on that name
# later.
#
# It took a fix to get here.  Before it, a third record showed up with this
# module squarely in the trace:
#
#     ngx_create_pool                        ngx_palloc.c:23
#     ngx_http_upstream_check_add_timers     ...check_module.c:1001
#     ngx_http_upstream_check_init_process   ...check_module.c:4090
#
# one pool per peer whose check type needs one.  The module only released
# them if a check handler happened to run while the process was shutting
# down, because that is the only path into need_exit.  It has an exit
# process handler now, and the record is gone.
#
# Note which cycle function the two below come through: Test::Nginx runs
# nginx with master_process off, so everything here is the single process
# path.  An entry for the master's own path could never be reached from
# this suite, and an entry nobody can reach is an entry nobody can check.
#
# If a later nginx brings a finding back, valgrind will say so with a full
# trace.  Add it then, with a name and a reason.

{
   nginx keeps the environment array until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_set_environment
   ...
}

{
   nginx keeps the connection and event arrays until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_event_process_init
   ...
}
